Documentation for the JSONAsObject format

In this format, a single JSON object is interpreted as a single [JSON](https://clickhouse.com/docs/reference/data-types/newjson) value. If the input has several JSON objects (comma separated), they are interpreted as separate rows. If the input data is enclosed in `[]`, it is interpreted as an array of JSONs.

This format can only be parsed for a table with a single field of type [JSON](https://clickhouse.com/docs/reference/data-types/newjson). The remaining columns must be set to [`DEFAULT`](https://clickhouse.com/docs/reference/statements/create/table#default) or [`MATERIALIZED`](https://clickhouse.com/docs/reference/statements/create/view#materialized-view).

**Example**

```sql
CREATE TABLE json_as_object (json JSON) ENGINE = Memory;
INSERT INTO json_as_object (json) FORMAT JSONAsObject {"foo":{"bar":{"x":"y"},"baz":1}},{},{"any json stucture":1}
SELECT * FROM json_as_object FORMAT JSONEachRow;
```

Documentation for the LineAsString format

The `LineAsString` format interprets every line of input data as a single string value. 
This format can only be parsed for a table with a single field of type [String](https://clickhouse.com/docs/reference/data-types/string).
The remaining columns must be set to [`DEFAULT`](https://clickhouse.com/docs/reference/statements/create/table#default), [`MATERIALIZED`](https://clickhouse.com/docs/reference/statements/create/view#materialized-view), or omitted.

**Example**

```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS line_as_string;
CREATE TABLE line_as_string (field String) ENGINE = Memory;
INSERT INTO line_as_string FORMAT LineAsString "I love apple", "I love banana", "I love orange";
SELECT * FROM line_as_string;
```

Documentation for the LineAsStringWithNames format

The `LineAsStringWithNames` format is similar to the [`LineAsString`](https://clickhouse.com/docs/reference/formats/LineAsString/LineAsString) format but prints the header row with column names.

**Example**

```sql
CREATE TABLE example (
    name String,
    value Int32
)
ENGINE = Memory;

INSERT INTO example VALUES ('John', 30), ('Jane', 25), ('Peter', 35);

SELECT * FROM example FORMAT LineAsStringWithNames;
```

Documentation for the LineAsStringWithNamesAndTypes format

The `LineAsStringWithNames` format is similar to the [`LineAsString`](https://clickhouse.com/docs/reference/formats/LineAsString/LineAsString) format
but prints two header rows: one with column names, the other with types.

**Example**

```sql
CREATE TABLE example (
    name String,
    value Int32
)
ENGINE = Memory;

INSERT INTO example VALUES ('John', 30), ('Jane', 25), ('Peter', 35);

SELECT * FROM example FORMAT LineAsStringWithNamesAndTypes;
```

Documentation for the RawBLOB format

The `RawBLOB` formats reads all input data to a single value. It is possible to parse only a table with a single field of type [`String`](https://clickhouse.com/docs/reference/data-types/string) or similar.
The result is output as a binary format without delimiters and escaping. If more than one value is output, the format is ambiguous, and it will be impossible to read the data back.

Below is a comparison of the formats `RawBLOB` and [`TabSeparatedRaw`](https://clickhouse.com/docs/reference/formats/TabSeparated/TabSeparatedRaw).

`RawBLOB`:
- data is output in binary format, no escaping;
- there are no delimiters between values;
- no new-line at the end of each value.

`TabSeparatedRaw`:
- data is output without escaping;
- the rows contain values separated by tabs;
- there is a line feed after the last val

